  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T05:57:16.144Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nAn employee earning purely compensation income from one employer is generally not required to file an annual Income Tax Return (ITR) if their taxable income does not exceed P250,000 and the income tax has been correctly withheld by the employer.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThis is governed by Section 51 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which states that individuals whose taxable income does not exceed P250,000 are not required to file an ITR, provided that their income tax has been withheld correctly.\n\n### Interpretation\n\n### Practical Meaning\nFor employees earning solely from one employer, this provision simplifies tax compliance, as they can rely on their employer to handle tax withholding. If their income is below the P250,000 threshold, they can avoid the administrative burden of filing an ITR. However, employees should ensure that their employer is correctly withholding taxes to maintain this exemption. If their income exceeds the threshold or if they have multiple sources of income, they must file an ITR to report their earnings and pay any additional tax due.",
